Let me look at the `read_idat_bytes` function again:

```c
static int read_idat_bytes(spng_ctx *ctx, uint32_t *bytes_read)
{
    ...
    while(!ctx->cur_chunk_bytes_left)
    {
        ret = read_header(ctx, NULL);
        if(ret) return ret;
        if(memcmp(ctx->current_chunk.type, type_idat, 4)) return SPNG_EIDAT_TOO_SHORT;
    }

    if(ctx->streaming)
    {
        len = SPNG_READ_SIZE;
        if(len > ctx->current_chunk.length) len = ctx->current_chunk.length;
    }
    else len = ctx->current_chunk.length;

    ret = read_chunk_bytes(ctx, len);
    *bytes_read = len;
    return ret;
}
```

Wait, there's a subtle issue here. After `read_header`, `ctx->cur_chunk_bytes_left` is set to the new chunk's length. But `read_header` also reads the CRC of the previous chunk and the next chunk header (8 bytes). So after `read_header`, `ctx->cur_chunk_bytes_left` is the length of the new chunk.

But what if the new chunk has length 0? Then `ctx->cur_chunk_bytes_left` is 0, and the while loop continues, reading the next header. This is fine.

What if the new chunk has a very large length? `chunk.length` is checked against `png_u32max` (2147483647) in `read_header`:
```c
if(chunk.length > png_u32max) return SPNG_ECHUNK_SIZE;
```

So `chunk.length` is at most 2147483647, which fits in `uint32_t`.

